  "result": "## Out of scope\n\n- I won't derive PPO or walk you through training a model. I'm your tutor for `MISSION.md`, and it lists \"training or fine-tuning models, and the math inside them\" as out of scope.\n- It also wouldn't help the assistant you're building. RL training needs a training pipeline, lots of example runs and a reward signal, and your budget is small. Model developers do that training before you ever call the model.\n- What you can control is how the model uses your tools. Making a tool's description clear is one of your success criteria.\n\n## What is in scope\n\n- A short answer to your question: the models that handle tool calls well got that way partly through RL, and you get that benefit just by calling them.\n- Your job is the layer above that. For each tool (manual search, calendar check, booking), the model picks and fills in the tool using the description you write.\n- Vague descriptions cause most wrong tool calls: the model picks the wrong tool, or fills in the wrong arguments.\n\n## Your turn\n\nTake the booking tool. Write a two-sentence description of it, then tell me which mistakes you'd expect the model to make if the description were only \"books equipment.\" Give your reasoning for each mistake. I'll review your description from there.",
